const BODY = {"1:Java History and Evolution": "<h4>Introduction</h4>\n<p>Java is a high-level, object-oriented, and platform-independent programming language developed by James Gosling and his team at Sun Microsystems in 1991. It was designed to create software that could run on different types of devices and operating systems.</p>\n<h4>History of Java</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Green Project (1991): Sun Microsystems initiated the Green Project to develop software for consumer electronic devices.</li><li>Oak Language: James Gosling created a language called Oak, named after an oak tree outside his office.</li><li>Renamed to Java (1995): Since Oak was already a registered trademark, the language was renamed Java.</li><li>First Release (1996): Java 1.0 was released with the slogan \"Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)\".</li><li>Oracle Acquisition (2010): Oracle Corporation acquired Sun Microsystems and became responsible for Java development.</li></ul>\n<h4>Evolution of Java</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Java 1.0 (1996): Introduced JVM, Applets, and AWT.</li><li>Java 1.1 (1997): Added JDBC, RMI, and Event Handling.</li><li>Java 2 (1998): Introduced Swing and Collections Framework.</li><li>Java 5 (2004): Added Generics, Autoboxing, Enums, and Annotations.</li><li>Java 7 (2011): Introduced Try-with-Resources and NIO.2.</li><li>Java 8 (2014): Added Lambda Expressions and Stream API.</li><li>Java 9 (2017): Introduced the Module System and JShell.</li><li>Java 11, 17, 21, and 25: Improved performance, security, cloud support, and modern programming features.</li></ul>\n<h4>Conclusion</h4>\n<p>Java has evolved from a small language called Oak into one of the world's most popular programming languages. Its platform independence, security, object-oriented nature, and continuous enhancements make it suitable for web, enterprise, mobile, and cloud applications. Key Point: Java follows the principle \"Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)\", allowing programs to run on any platform with a Java Virtual Machine (JVM).</p>", "1:Java Features": "<p>Java is a high-level, object-oriented programming language developed by James Gosling and his team at Sun Microsystems in 1995.</p>\n<h4>Java provides several powerful features:</h4>\n<h4>1. Platform Independent</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Java programs run on any system with a JVM.</li></ul>\n<h4>2. Object-Oriented</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Based on classes and objects.</li><li>Supports encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction.</li></ul>\n<h4>3. Simple</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Similar syntax to C/C++ but removes complex features like pointers.</li></ul>\n<h4>4. Secure</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>No direct memory access.</li><li>Bytecode verification improves security.</li></ul>\n<h4>5. Robust</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Strong memory management and exception handling.</li></ul>\n<h4>6. Multithreaded</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Supports concurrent execution of multiple threads.</li></ul>\n<h4>7. Distributed</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Supports networking and internet-based applications.</li></ul>\n<h4>8. High Performance</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Uses Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler for faster execution.</li></ul>\n<h4>9. Portable</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Same bytecode works on different operating systems.</li></ul>\n<h4>10. Dynamic</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Supports dynamic loading of classes during runtime.</li></ul>\n<h4>Conclusion:</h4>\n<p>These features make Java suitable for Desktop,enterprise, web, cloud, and mobile applications.</p>\n<h4>Java's</h4>", "1:Java's Magic: Byte Code": "<h4>Introduction</h4>\n<p>One of the most important features of Java is Byte Code. It enables Java programs to run on different operating systems without modification, giving Java its famous \"Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)\" capability.</p>\n<h4>What is Byte Code?</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Byte code is an intermediate code generated by the Java compiler (javac).</li><li>It is neither machine code nor source code.</li><li>Byte code is stored in a .class file.</li><li>It is platform-independent and can run on any system that has a Java Virtual Machine</li></ul>\n<p>(JVM).</p>\n<h4>How Byte Code is Generated</h4>\n<p>Java Source Code (.java)</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>javac Compiler</code></pre></div>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>Byte Code (.class)</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>JVM</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>Machine Code</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<h4>Execution</h4>\n<h4>Working of Byte Code</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Programmer writes Java source code (.java file).</li><li>The Java compiler (javac) compiles the source code into byte code (.class file).</li><li>The JVM loads the byte code.</li><li>The JVM interprets or uses the Just-In-Time (JIT) Compiler to convert byte code into machine code.</li><li>The machine code is executed by the operating system.</li></ul>\n<h4>Advantages of Byte Code</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Platform Independence: Runs on any operating system with a JVM.</li><li>Portability: Same .class file can be executed on different platforms.</li><li>Security: JVM verifies byte code before execution.</li><li>Performance: JIT compiler optimizes byte code for faster execution.</li><li>WORA Support: Write Once, Run Anywhere.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class HelloWorld {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello World\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Compilation:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>javac HelloWorld.java</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">HelloWorld.class ← Byte Code\nExecution:</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>java HelloWorld</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Conclusion</h4>\n<p>Byte code is Java's \"magic\" because it makes Java platform-independent. The Java compiler converts source code into byte code, and the JVM executes it on any operating system, enabling the Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA) feature of Java. Key Point: Byte Code is the intermediate, platform-independent code that allows Java programs to run on any system with a JVM.</p>", "1:How Java differs from C and C++": "<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Java</th><th>C</th><th>C++</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Programming Paradigm</th><td>Purely Object-Oriented</td><td>Procedural</td><td>Multi-paradigm (Procedural + OOP)</td></tr><tr><th>Platform Independence</th><td>Yes (JVM-based)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><th>Compilation</th><td>Compiles to Bytecode and runs on JVM</td><td>Compiles to Machine Code</td><td>Compiles to Machine Code</td></tr><tr><th>Memory Management</th><td>Automatic Garbage Collection</td><td>Manual (malloc/free)</td><td>Manual (new/delete)</td></tr><tr><th>Pointers</th><td>No direct pointer support</td><td>Supports pointers</td><td>Supports pointers</td></tr><tr><th>Multiple Inheritance</th><td>Not supported through classes; achieved using interfaces</td><td>Not supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><th>Friend Functions</th><td>Not supported</td><td>Not supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><th>Header Files</th><td>No header files</td><td>Uses header files (.h)</td><td>Uses header files (.h)</td></tr><tr><th>Preprocessor Directives</th><td>No preprocessor</td><td>Supports #include, #define</td><td>Supports #include, #define</td></tr><tr><th>Security</th><td>Highly secure</td><td>Less secure</td><td>Moderate security</td></tr><tr><th>Exception Handling</th><td>Built-in exception handling</td><td>Not available</td><td>Available</td></tr><tr><th>Multithreading</th><td>Built-in support</td><td>Limited support</td><td>Supported through libraries</td></tr><tr><th>Dynamic Binding</th><td>Available by default</td><td>Not supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><th>Portability</th><td>High (Write Once, Run Anywhere)</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><th>Method Overriding</th><td>Supported</td><td>Not supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><th>Structures and Unions</th><td>Not supported as separate entities</td><td>Supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><th>Global Variables</th><td>Not supported directly</td><td>Supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><th>Destructors</th><td>Not available</td><td>Not available</td><td>Available</td></tr><tr><th>Runtime Environment</th><td>JVM required</td><td>No runtime required</td><td>No runtime required</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4>Advantages of Java over C and C++</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Platform Independence (Write Once, Run Anywhere)</li><li>Automatic Memory Management</li><li>Enhanced Security</li><li>Built-in Multithreading</li><li>Robust Exception Handling</li><li>Rich Standard Library</li><li>Better Support for Network and Enterprise Applications</li></ul>\n<h4>Conclusion</h4>\n<p>Java was designed by James Gosling to overcome many limitations of C and C++. By removing complex features such as pointers, manual memory management, and multiple inheritance, Java provides a safer, more portable, and easier-to-maintain programming environment. As a result, Java is widely used in enterprise applications, web services, cloud computing, and Android development.</p>", "1:Principles of OOP": "<p>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that organizes software around objects rather than functions and logic. The four fundamental principles of OOP are Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, and Polymorphism.</p>\n<h4>1. Encapsulation</h4>\n<p>Definition: Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data (variables) and methods (functions) into a single unit called a class and restricting direct access to the data.</p>\n<h4>Advantages:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Provides data security.</li><li>Prevents unauthorized access.</li><li>Improves maintainability.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    private int marks;\n    public void setMarks(int m) {\n        marks = m;\n    }\n    public int getMarks() {\n        return marks;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Abstraction</h4>\n<p>Definition: Abstraction hides implementation details and shows only the essential features of an object.</p>\n<h4>Advantages:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Reduces complexity.</li><li>Enhances security.</li><li>Simplifies program design.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>abstract class Vehicle {\n    abstract void start();\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>3. Inheritance</h4>\n<p>Definition: Inheritance allows a class to acquire the properties and methods of another class.</p>\n<h4>Advantages:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Promotes code reusability.</li><li>Reduces code duplication.</li><li>Supports hierarchical classification.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Eating\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() {\n        System.out.println(\"Barking\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>4. Polymorphism</h4>\n<p>Definition: Polymorphism means \"many forms.\" A single interface can be used to represent different types of actions.</p>\n<h4>Types:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Compile-Time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)</li></ul>\n<h4>2. Run-Time Polymorphism (Method Overriding)</h4>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Shape {\n    void draw() {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing Shape\");\n    }\n}\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    void draw() {\n        System.out.println(\"Drawing Circle\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Summary of OOP Principles</h4>\n<h4>Principle                   Meaning                     Benefit</h4>\n<h4>Encapsulation   Wrapping data and methods into a class   Security</h4>\n<h4>Abstraction     Hiding implementation details            Simplicity</h4>\n<h4>Inheritance     Acquiring properties of another class    Reusability</h4>\n<h4>Polymorphism    One interface, many forms                Flexibility</h4>\n<h4>Conclusion</h4>\n<p>The four principles of OOP—Encapsulation, Abstraction, Inheritance, and Polymorphism— help in developing secure, reusable, modular, and maintainable software systems. These principles form the foundation of Java and other object-oriented programming languages.</p>", "1:Two Paradigms": "<p>A programming paradigm is a style or approach used to solve programming problems.</p>\n<h4>1. Procedural Programming Paradigm</h4>\n<p>Procedural Programming organizes a program as a collection of procedures or functions that operate on data.</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Program is divided into functions or procedures.</li><li>Focuses on sequence of actions.</li><li>Data and functions are separate.</li><li>Suitable for small applications.</li></ul>\n<h4>Examples: C, Pascal</h4>\n<h4>Features:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Top-down approach</li><li>Functions perform operations on data</li><li>Less secure due to global data access</li></ul>\n<h4>Advantages:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Simple and easy to understand.</li><li>Faster execution for small programs.</li><li>Less memory consumption.</li></ul>\n<h4>Disadvantages:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Difficult to maintain large programs.</li><li>Limited code reusability.</li><li>Poor data security.</li></ul>\n<h4>2. Object-Oriented Programming Paradigm</h4>\n<p>Object-Oriented Programming organizes a program as a collection of objects that contain both data and methods.</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Program is divided into objects and classes.</li><li>Focuses on data and behaviour together.</li><li>Provides security through encapsulation.</li><li>Suitable for large and complex applications.</li></ul>\n<h4>Examples: Java, C++, C#, Python</h4>\n<h4>Features:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Bottom-up approach</li><li>Supports inheritance and polymorphism</li><li>High code reusability and maintainability</li></ul>\n<h4>Advantages:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>High code reusability.</li><li>Better security through encapsulation.</li><li>Easy maintenance and modification.</li><li>Models real-world entities effectively.</li></ul>\n<h4>Disadvantages:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Requires more memory.</li><li>More complex than procedural programming.</li></ul>\n<h4>Difference between Procedural and OOP</h4>\n<h4>Procedural Programming             Object-Oriented Programming</h4>\n<h4>Function-oriented                      Object-oriented</h4>\n<h4>Top-down approach                      Bottom-up approach</h4>\n<p>Data and functions are separate        Data and methods are combined</p>\n<h4>Less secure                            More secure</h4>\n<p>Difficult to maintain large programs   Easy to maintain large programs Limited reusability                    High reusability through inheritance</p>\n<h4>Conclusion</h4>\n<p>Object-Oriented Programming is a modern programming paradigm that models real-world entities as objects. Compared to procedural programming, OOP provides better security, reusability, maintainability, and scalability, making it suitable for developing large software applications such as Java-based systems.</p>", "1:A First Simple Program": "<p>Add Two Numbers:</p>\n<h4>****************</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>import java.util.Scanner;\npublic class AddNumbers {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        System.out.print(\"Enter first number: \");\n        int a = sc.nextInt();\n        System.out.print(\"Enter second number: \");\n        int b = sc.nextInt();\n        int sum = a + b;\n        System.out.println(\"Sum = \" + sum);\n        sc.close();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Enter first number: 25\nEnter second number: 35\nSum = 60</pre>\n<h4>Common Scanner Methods</h4>\n<p>Method                       Purpose                               Example</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>nextInt()                  Reads an integer        int age = sc.nextInt();</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Reads a decimal num-</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>nextDouble()                                       double salary = sc.nextDouble();</code></pre></div>\n<p>ber</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>nextFloat()                Reads a float           float marks = sc.nextFloat();\nnext()                     Reads a single word     String city = sc.next();\nnextLine()                 Reads a complete line   String address = sc.nextLine();\nnextBoolean()              Reads true or false     boolean status = sc.nextBoolean();\nnextLong()                 Reads a long integer    long mobile = sc.nextLong();</code></pre></div>", "1:Two Control Statements": "<p>Java provides control statements to control the flow of program execution. Two important control statements are:</p>\n<h4>1. Selection Control Statement (if-else)</h4>\n<p>The if-else statement is used to make decisions based on a condition.</p>\n<h4>Syntax:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>if (condition) {\n    // statements executed if condition is true\n} else {\n    // statements executed if condition is false\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class IfElseExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int marks = 75;\n        if (marks &gt;= 50) {\n            System.out.println(\"Pass\");\n        } else {\n            System.out.println(\"Fail\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Pass</pre>\n<h4>Explanation:</h4>\n<p>If marks is 50 or more, \"Pass\" is displayed; otherwise, \"Fail\" is displayed.</p>\n<h4>2. Iteration Control Statement (for Loop)</h4>\n<p>The for loop is used to execute a block of code repeatedly for a specified number of times.</p>\n<h4>Syntax:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>for (initialization; condition; update) {\n    // statements\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class ForLoopExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {\n            System.out.println(\"Count: \" + i);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Count: 1\nCount: 2\nCount: 3\nCount: 4\nCount: 5</pre>\n<h4>Explanation:</h4>\n<p>The loop starts with i = 1, executes until i &lt;= 5, and increments i by 1 after each iteration.</p>\n<h4>Conclusion</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>if-else is used for decision-making.</li><li>for loop is used for repetitive execution of statements.</li></ul>", "1:Java keywords": "<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>Keywords in Java are reserved words that have predefined meanings and cannot be used as identifiers such as variable names, class names, or method names.</p>\n<h4>Features of Java Keywords</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Keywords are reserved by the Java language.</li><li>They have special meanings and purposes.</li><li>They cannot be used as identifiers.</li><li>Java keywords are case-sensitive.</li><li>Java has around 50 reserved keywords.</li></ul>\n<h4>Common Java Keywords and Their Uses</h4>\n<p>Keyword                    Purpose class          Declares a class public         Specifies public access private        Specifies private access Belongs to the class rather than obstatic jects void           Indicates no return value int            Declares an integer variable if             Decision-making statement else           Alternative block for if for            Looping statement while          Repetition statement return         Returns a value from a method new            Creates an object this           Refers to the current object extends        Inherits a class implements     Implements an interface</p>\n<h4>Example Program Using Keywords</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class KeywordDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int num = 10;\n        if (num &gt; 0) {\n            System.out.println(\"Positive Number\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Explanation</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>public, class, static, void, int, and if are Java keywords.</li><li>These keywords define the structure and behavior of the program.</li></ul>", "1:The Primitive Types": "<p>A Data Type in Java specifies the type of value a variable can store and the amount of memory allocated to it.</p>\n<h4>Java data types are classified into two categories:</h4>\n<p>Primitive data types are predefined by Java and store simple values. Non-primitive data types are created by the programmer or provided by Java libraries. They store references (addresses) of objects.</p>\n<h4>Primitive Data Types</h4>\n<p>Primitive types are the fundamental data types that store single values. Java defines eight primitive data types, summarized below:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Default</th><th>Size</th><th>Example</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>boolean</th><td>Logical values</td><td>false</td><td>JVM-dependent (typically 1 byte)</td><td>true, false</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><th>char</th><td>16-bit Unicode character</td><td>\\u0000</td><td>2 bytes</td><td>'A', '\\u0041'</td><td>0 to 65,535</td></tr><tr><th>byte</th><td>8-bit signed integer</td><td>0</td><td>1 byte</td><td>10</td><td>-128 to 127</td></tr><tr><th>short</th><td>16-bit signed integer</td><td>0</td><td>2 bytes</td><td>2000</td><td>-32,768 to 32,767</td></tr><tr><th>int</th><td>32-bit signed integer</td><td>0</td><td>4 bytes</td><td>1000, -500</td><td>-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647</td></tr><tr><th>long</th><td>64-bit signed integer</td><td>0L</td><td>8 bytes</td><td>123456789L</td><td>±9.22e18</td></tr><tr><th>float</th><td>32-bit floating point</td><td>0.0f</td><td>4 bytes</td><td>3.14f</td><td>~6–7 digits precision</td></tr><tr><th>double</th><td>64-bit floating point</td><td>0.0d</td><td>8 bytes</td><td>3.14159d</td><td>~15–16 digits precision</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Memory Hierarchy of Primitive Data Types byte (1 byte) ↓ short (2 bytes) ↓ int (4 bytes) ↓ long (8 bytes) float (4 bytes) ↓ double (8 bytes)</p>\n<h4>Non-Primitive Data Types in Java</h4>\n<h4>1. String</h4>\n<p>A String is a sequence of characters used to store text.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>String name = \"Ramesh\";\nSystem.out.println(name);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Ramesh\n2. Array</pre>\n<p>An array stores multiple values of the same type in a single variable.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int marks[] = {80, 85, 90};\nSystem.out.println(marks[0]);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">80\n3. Class</pre>\n<p>A class is a blueprint for creating objects.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    String name;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>4. Object</h4>\n<p>An object is an instance of a class.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Student s = new Student();\ns.name = \"Ravi\";</code></pre></div>\n<h4>5. Interface</h4>\n<p>An interface contains abstract methods that must be implemented by classes.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>interface Vehicle {\n    void start();\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>6. Enum</h4>\n<p>Enum represents a fixed set of constants.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>enum Day {</code></pre></div>\n<h4>MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Primitive vs Non-Primitive Data Types</h4>\n<h4>Primitive Data Types             Non-Primitive Data Types</h4>\n<h4>Store actual values          Store object references</h4>\n<h4>Fixed size                   Size varies</h4>\n<h4>Predefined by Java           Created by programmer or Java API</h4>\n<h4>Cannot call methods          Can call methods</h4>\n<h4>Examples: int, float, char   Examples: String, Array, Class, Object</h4>", "1:Booleans": "<ul class=\"k\"><li>Used to store logical values.</li><li>Can have only two values: true or false.</li><li>Commonly used in decision-making statements.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>boolean result = true;\nSystem.out.println(result);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">true</pre>", "1:Characters": "<ul class=\"k\"><li>Stores a single character.</li><li>Uses Unicode encoding.</li><li>Occupies 2 bytes of memory.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>char grade = 'A';\nchar symbol = '@';\nSystem.out.println(grade);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">A</pre>", "1:Integers": "<h4>3. byte</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Smallest integer data type.</li><li>Useful for saving memory in large arrays.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>byte age = 25;\nSystem.out.println(age);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">25\n4. short</pre>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Used when integer values are small.</li><li>Larger range than byte.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>short salary = 20000;\nSystem.out.println(salary);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">20000\n5. int</pre>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Most commonly used integer type.</li><li>Suitable for counting and indexing operations.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int marks = 950;\nSystem.out.println(marks);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">950\n6. long</pre>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Used when values exceed the range of int.</li><li>Must end with L or l.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>long population = 1400000000L;\nSystem.out.println(population);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">1400000000</pre>", "1:Floating-Point Types": "<h4>7. float</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Stores decimal numbers with single precision.</li><li>Must end with f or F.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>float price = 99.99f;\nSystem.out.println(price);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">99.99\n8. double</pre>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Stores decimal numbers with double precision.</li><li>More accurate than float.</li><li>Default type for decimal values.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>double pi = 3.1415926535;\nSystem.out.println(pi);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">3.1415926535</pre>", "1:Variables": "<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>A variable is a named memory location used to store data values during program execution. The value of a variable can change while the program is running.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>dataType variableName = value;</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int age = 20;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here, int is the data type, age is the variable name, and 20 is the value stored in the variable.</p>\n<h4>Types of Variables in Java</h4>\n<h4>1. Local Variables</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Declared inside a method, constructor, or block.</li><li>Accessible only within that method or block.</li><li>Must be initialized before use.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class LocalVariableDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int num = 10;\n        System.out.println(num);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Instance Variables</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Declared inside a class but outside methods.</li><li>Each object has its own copy of instance variables.</li><li>Initialized with default values if not assigned.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    int id;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>3. Static Variables</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Declared using the static keyword.</li><li>Shared by all objects of the class.</li><li>Memory is allocated only once.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    static String college = \"VRSEC\";\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Rules for Naming Variables</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Variable names can contain letters, digits, _, and $.</li><li>Variable names cannot start with a digit.</li><li>Keywords cannot be used as variable names.</li><li>Variable names are case-sensitive.</li><li>Use meaningful names for better readability.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example Program</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class VariableDemo {\n    static String college = \"VRSEC\";\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int age = 20; // Local variable\n        System.out.println(\"Age: \" + age);\n        System.out.println(\"College: \" + college);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Age: 20\nCollege: VRSEC</pre>", "1:Type Conversion and Casting": "<h4>Introduction</h4>\n<p>In Java, Type Conversion and Type Casting are used to convert a value from one data type to another. This is essential when performing operations involving different data types.</p>\n<h4>1. Type Conversion (Widening Conversion)</h4>\n<p>Type conversion is the automatic conversion of a smaller data type into a larger data type by the Java compiler. It is also called implicit casting or widening conversion. Order of Widening Conversion byte → short → int → long → float → double char → int → long → float → double</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class TypeConversionDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int num = 100;\n        double d = num; // Automatic conversion\n        System.out.println(\"Integer Value: \" + num);\n        System.out.println(\"Double Value: \" + d);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Integer Value: 100\nDouble Value: 100.0</pre>\n<h4>Explanation:</h4>\n<p>The integer value is automatically converted into a double value without any data loss.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class CharToIntDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        char ch = 'A';\n        int num = ch; // Automatic conversion\n        System.out.println(\"Character: \" + ch);\n        System.out.println(\"Integer Value: \" + num);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Character: A\nInteger Value: 65\n2. Type Casting (Narrowing Conversion)</pre>\n<p>Type casting is the manual conversion of a larger data type into a smaller data type. It is also called explicit casting or narrowing conversion. Order of Narrowing Conversion double → float → long → int → short → byte double → float → long → int → char</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>targetType variable = (targetType) value;</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class TypeCastingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        double d = 99.99;\n        int num = (int) d; // Explicit casting\n        System.out.println(\"Double Value: \" + d);\n        System.out.println(\"Integer Value: \" + num);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Double Value: 99.99\nInteger Value: 99</pre>\n<h4>Explanation:</h4>\n<p>The fractional part (.99) is lost when converting a double to an int.</p>\n<h4>Differences Between Type Conversion and Type Casting</h4>\n<h4>Type Conversion               Type Casting</h4>\n<h4>Automatic process          Manual process</h4>\n<h4>Performed by program-</h4>\n<p>Performed by compiler mer</p>\n<h4>No data loss               May result in data loss</h4>\n<p>Smaller type to larger     Larger type to smaller type                       type</p>\n<h4>Also called Widening       Also called Narrowing</h4>", "1:Automatic Type Promotion in Expressions": "<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>Automatic Type Promotion in Java is the process where the Java compiler automatically converts smaller data types into larger data types while evaluating an expression. This helps prevent data loss during arithmetic operations.</p>\n<h4>Promotion Rules</h4>\n<p>Java promotes operands according to the following order: byte → short → int → long → float → double char → int → long → float → double</p>\n<h4>Example 1: byte + byte</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Example1 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        byte a = 10;\n        byte b = 20;\n        int c = a + b; // byte is promoted to int\n        System.out.println(c);\n    }\n}\nbyte c = a + b;   // Error Reason: a + b returns an int, not a byte.</code></pre></div>\n<p>In Java, all arithmetic operations (+, -, *, /, %) on byte, short, and char are automatically promoted to int before the operation is performed.</p>\n<h4>Example 2: short + short</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>short x = 100;\nshort y = 200;\nint z = x + y;\nSystem.out.println(z);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example 3: char + char</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>char ch1 = 'A';\nchar ch2 = 1;\nint result = ch1 + ch2;\nSystem.out.println(result);\nSystem.out.println((char) result);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example 4: int + long</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int a = 100;\nlong b = 200L;\nlong c = a + b;\nSystem.out.println(c);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example 5: int + float</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int a = 25;\nfloat b = 2.5f;\nfloat c = a + b;\nSystem.out.println(c);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example 6: long + float</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>long a = 100L;\nfloat b = 20.5f;\nfloat c = a + b;\nSystem.out.println(c);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example 7: float + double</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>float a = 15.5f;\ndouble b = 10.25;\ndouble c = a + b;\nSystem.out.println(c);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example 8: Mixed Data Types</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>byte b = 5;\nshort s = 10;\nchar c = 'A';\nint i = 20;\nfloat f = 2.5f;\nfloat result = b + s + c + i + f;\nSystem.out.println(result);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example 9: Division</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int a = 5;\nint b = 2;\nSystem.out.println(a / b);          // 2</code></pre></div>\n<p>Since both operands are int, the result is an int.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>System.out.println((float) a / b); //2.5</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operands</th><th>Promoted Type</th><th>Result Type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>byte + byte</th><td>int</td><td>int</td></tr><tr><th>byte + short</th><td>int</td><td>int</td></tr><tr><th>short + short</th><td>int</td><td>int</td></tr><tr><th>char + char</th><td>int</td><td>int</td></tr><tr><th>int + long</th><td>long</td><td>long</td></tr><tr><th>long + float</th><td>float</td><td>float</td></tr><tr><th>float + double</th><td>double</td><td>double</td></tr><tr><th>int + double</th><td>double</td><td>double</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "1:Arrays": "<p>An array in Java is a container object that stores a fixed number of values of the same data type. Each value is stored at an index, starting from 0.</p>\n<h4>1. Declaring an Array</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[] numbers;</code></pre></div>\n<p>or</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int numbers[];</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Creating an Array</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[] numbers = new int[5];</code></pre></div>\n<p>This creates an array that can hold 5 integers.</p>\n<h4>Default values:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>int → 0</li><li>double → 0.0</li><li>boolean → false</li><li>char → '\\u0000'</li><li>Objects → null</li></ul>\n<h4>3. Initializing an Array</h4>\n<h4>Method 1:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Method 2:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[] numbers = new int[5];\nnumbers[0] = 10;\nnumbers[1] = 20;\nnumbers[2] = 30;\nnumbers[3] = 40;\nnumbers[4] = 50;</code></pre></div>\n<h4>4. Accessing Array Elements</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>System.out.println(numbers[0]); // Output: 10\nSystem.out.println(numbers[3]); // Output: 40</code></pre></div>\n<h4>5. Modifying Elements</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>numbers[2] = 100;\nSystem.out.println(numbers[2]); // Output: 100</code></pre></div>\n<h4>6. Finding the Length</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>System.out.println(numbers.length);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">5\n7. Looping Through an Array\nUsing a for loop:</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\nfor (int i = 0; i &lt; numbers.length; i++) {\n    System.out.println(numbers[i]);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">10\n20\n30\n40\n50\nUsing a for-each loop:</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>for (int num : numbers) {\n    System.out.println(num);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>8. Multidimensional Arrays</h4>\n<h4>Example of a 2D array:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[][] matrix = {\n    {1, 2, 3},\n    {4, 5, 6},\n    {7, 8, 9}\n};\nSystem.out.println(matrix[1][2]); // Output: 6</code></pre></div>\n<h4>9. Common Operations</h4>\n<h4>Find the sum</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40};\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int num : arr) {\n    sum += num;\n}\nSystem.out.println(sum);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">100\nFind the largest element</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int[] arr = {5, 12, 8, 20, 3};\nint max = arr[0];\nfor (int i = 1; i &lt; arr.length; i++) {\n    if (arr[i] &gt; max) {\n        max = arr[i];\n    }\n}\nSystem.out.println(max);</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">20</pre>\n<h4>Advantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Fast access using indexes (O(1)).</li><li>Easy to store multiple values of the same type.</li><li>Efficient memory usage.</li></ul>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Fixed size (cannot grow or shrink after creation).</li><li>Can only store one data type.</li><li>Inserting or deleting elements is not convenient.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example Program</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class ArrayExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] marks = {85, 90, 78, 92, 88};\n        System.out.println(\"Marks:\");\n        for (int mark : marks) {\n            System.out.println(mark);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Total subjects: \" + marks.length);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Marks:\n85\n90\n78\n92\n88\nTotal subjects: 5</pre>\n<h4>Key Points to Remember</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Arrays store elements of the same type.</li><li>Array indexing starts at 0.</li><li>The size of an array is fixed once created.</li><li>Use .length to get the array size.</li><li>Access elements using array[index].</li></ul>", "1:Operators": "<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>Operators in Java are special symbols used to perform operations on variables and values.</p>\n<h4>1. Arithmetic Operators</h4>\n<p>Used to perform mathematical calculations. Operator      Description          Example +        Addition                  a+b -        Subtraction               a-b *        Multiplication            a*b</p>\n<h4>/        Division                  a/b</h4>\n<p>Modulus (Remain- %                                  a%b der)</p>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int a = 20, b = 10;\nSystem.out.println(a + b); // 30\nSystem.out.println(a % b); // 0</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Unary Operators</h4>\n<p>Operate on a single operand. Operator Description +        Unary plus -        Unary minus ++       Increment --       Decrement</p>\n<h4>!        Logical NOT</h4>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<h4>Example Program</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class UnaryExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int a = 10;\n        boolean flag = true;\n        System.out.println(\"Unary Plus (+a): \" + (+a)); // 10\n        System.out.println(\"Unary Minus (-a): \" + (-a)); // -10\n        System.out.println(\"Pre Increment (++a): \" + (++a)); // 11\n        System.out.println(\"Post Increment (a++): \" + (a++)); // 11\n        System.out.println(\"Value after a++: \" + a);      // 12\n        System.out.println(\"Pre Decrement (--a): \" + (--a)); // 11\n        System.out.println(\"Post Decrement (a--): \" + (a--)); // 11\n        System.out.println(\"Value after a--: \" + a);     // 10\n        System.out.println(\"Logical NOT (!flag): \" + (!flag)); // false\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>3. Assignment Operators</h4>\n<p>Used to assign values to variables. Operator Example =        a = 10 +=       a += 5 -=       a -= 5 *=       a *= 2 /=       a /= 2</p>\n<h4>%=       a %= 3</h4>\n<h4>4. Relational (Comparison) Operators</h4>\n<p>Compare two values and return true or false. Operator        Meaning ==       Equal to !=       Not equal to &gt;        Greater than</p>\n<h4>&lt;        Less than</h4>\n<p>Greater than or equal &gt;= to</p>\n<h4>&lt;=       Less than or equal to</h4>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int a = 10, b = 20;\nSystem.out.println(a &lt; b); // true</code></pre></div>\n<h4>5. Logical Operators</h4>\n<p>Used to combine multiple conditions. Operator Meaning &amp;&amp;       Logical AND ||       Logical OR</p>\n<h4>!        Logical NOT</h4>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int a = 10, b = 20;\nSystem.out.println(a &lt; b &amp;&amp; b &gt; 15); // true</code></pre></div>\n<h4>6. Ternary (Conditional) Operator</h4>\n<p>A shorthand for the if-else statement.</p>\n<h4>Syntax:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>condition ? expression1 : expression2;</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>int a = 10, b = 20;\nint max = (a &gt; b) ? a : b;\nSystem.out.println(max); // 20</code></pre></div>\n<h4>7. instanceof Operator</h4>\n<p>Checks whether an object belongs to a particular class.</p>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>String s = \"Java\";\nSystem.out.println(s instanceof String); // true</code></pre></div>", "2:Class Fundamentals": "<p>A class is a blueprint or template for creating objects. It defines the properties (variables) and behaviors (methods) of an object.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class ClassName {\n    // Variables (Data Members)\n    // Methods (Member Functions)\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    int id;\n    String name;\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(id + \" \" + name);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>In the above example:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Student is the class.</li><li>id and name are instance variables.</li><li>display() is an instance method.</li></ul>\n<h4>Instance variables and Local variables java</h4>\n<h4>Instance Variable</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Declared inside a class, outside methods.</li><li>Memory allocated when an object is created.</li><li>Can be accessed by all instance methods.</li></ul>\n<h4>Local Variable</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Declared inside methods, constructors, or blocks.</li><li>Memory allocated when the method is called.</li><li>Cannot be accessed outside the method.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    // Instance Variables\n    int rollNo = 101;\n    String name = \"Ramesh\";\n    void display() {\n        // Local Variable\n        int marks = 95;\n        System.out.println(\"Roll No : \" + rollNo);\n        System.out.println(\"Name : \" + name);\n        System.out.println(\"Marks : \" + marks);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s = new Student();\n        s.display();\n    }\n}\nclass Demo {\n    // Instance Variable\n    int x = 10;\n    void method() {\n        // Local Variable\n        int y = 20;\n        System.out.println(\"Instance Variable x = \" + x);\n        System.out.println(\"Local Variable y = \" + y);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Demo d = new Demo();\n        d.method();\n        // System.out.println(y); // Error: y cannot be accessed here\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>", "2:Declaring Objects": "<p>An object is an instance of a class.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>ClassName objectName;</code></pre></div>\n<p>This only declares a reference variable. It does not create an object.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Student s;</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Here:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>s is an object reference variable.</li><li>No memory is allocated yet.</li></ul>\n<h4>3. Creating an Object</h4>\n<p>To create an object, use the new keyword.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>ClassName objectName = new ClassName();</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Student s = new Student();</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Here:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>new Student() creates the object.</li><li>Memory is allocated.</li><li>s stores the reference (address) of the object.</li></ul>", "2:Assigning Object Reference Variables": "<p>In Java, objects are not stored directly in variables. Instead, variables store references (memory addresses) to objects.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>ClassName referenceVariable = new ClassName();</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>ClassName → Name of the class.</li><li>referenceVariable → Holds the reference to the object.</li><li>new ClassName() → Creates a new object in memory.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example 1: Creating and Assigning an Object</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    String name;\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student(); // Object creation\n        s1.name = \"Ramesh\";\n        System.out.println(\"Student Name: \" + s1.name);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Student Name: Ramesh</pre>\n<h4>Explanation</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>new Student() creates a Student object.</li><li>s1 stores the reference to that object.</li><li>Using s1.name, we access the object's data.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example 2: Assigning One Reference Variable to Another</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    String name;\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student();\n        s1.name = \"Ramesh\";\n        Student s2 = s1; // Copying reference\n        System.out.println(s1.name);\n        System.out.println(s2.name);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Ramesh\nRamesh</pre>\n<h4>Explanation</h4>\n<p>Before assignment: s1 --------&gt; Student Object name = \"Ramesh\"</p>\n<h4>After:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Student s2 = s1;</code></pre></div>\n<p>s1 ----\\ \\ ---&gt; Student Object / s2 ----/ Both s1 and s2 refer to the same object.</p>\n<h4>Example 3: Modifying Through Another Reference</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    String name;\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student();\n        s1.name = \"Ramesh\";\n        Student s2 = s1;\n        s2.name = \"Krishna\";\n        System.out.println(s1.name);\n        System.out.println(s2.name);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Krishna\nKrishna</pre>\n<p>Both variables point to the same object, so changing through one reference affects the same object.</p>\n<h4>Example 4: Creating a New Object</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    String name;\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student();\n        s1.name = \"Ramesh\";\n        Student s2 = new Student();\n        s2.name = \"Krishna\";\n        System.out.println(s1.name);\n        System.out.println(s2.name);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Ramesh\nKrishna</pre>\n<p>Here, s1 and s2 refer to different objects.</p>\n<h4>Example 5: Multiple Reference Variables</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Employee {\n    String name;\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Employee e1 = new Employee();\n        e1.name = \"Anil\";\n        Employee e2 = e1;\n        Employee e3 = e2;\n        System.out.println(e1.name);\n        System.out.println(e2.name);\n        System.out.println(e3.name);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Anil\nAnil\nAnil</pre>\n<p>All three variables point to the same object.</p>\n<h4>Advantages of Object Reference Variables</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Efficient memory usage by sharing objects.</li><li>Enables object-oriented programming concepts like method calls and object interaction.</li><li>Multiple references can access the same object.</li><li>Supports dynamic object creation during program execution.</li></ul>", "2:Introducing Methods": "<p>Definition:</p>\n<h4>***********</h4>\n<p>A method in Java is a block of code that performs a specific task. Methods help organize programs into smaller, reusable, and manageable units. Instead of writing the same code multiple times, a method can be called whenever needed.</p>\n<h4>Java provides two types of methods:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Predefined (Built-in) Methods – Provided by Java libraries (e.g., System.out.println(), Math.sqrt()).</li><li>User-defined Methods – Created by programmers to perform specific tasks.</li></ul>\n<h4>Advantages of Methods</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Promotes code reusability.</li><li>Reduces code duplication.</li><li>Makes programs easy to understand and maintain.</li><li>Improves modularity.</li><li>Simplifies debugging and testing.</li></ul>\n<p>Syntax of a Method access_modifier return_type methodName(parameters)</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>{\n    // Method body\n    return value; // Optional for void methods\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Components of a Method</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Access Modifier – Specifies visibility (public, private, protected).</li><li>public → Everyone can access</li><li>private → Only this class can access</li><li>protected → Same package + subclasses can access</li><li>default → Only same package can access</li><li>Return Type – Specifies the type of value returned (int, double, String, void, etc.).</li><li>Method Name – Identifier used to call the method.</li><li>Parameters – Input values passed to the method.</li><li>Method Body – Contains the statements to execute.</li><li>Return Statement – Returns a value to the calling method (not required for void methods).</li></ul>\n<h4>Example Program</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class MethodDemo {\n    // User-defined method\n    static int add(int a, int b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int result = add(10, 20);\n        System.out.println(\"Sum = \" + result);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Sum = 30</pre>\n<h4>Types of User-defined Methods</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Method with No Arguments and No Return Value</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Demo {\n    static void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Welcome to Java\");\n    }\n    public static void main(String args[]) {\n        display();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Method with Arguments and Return Value</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Square {\n    static int square(int n) {\n        return n * n;\n    }\n    public static void main(String args[]) {\n        System.out.println(square(5));\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">25</pre>\n<h4>Method Calling</h4>\n<p>A method is executed when it is called.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>methodName(arguments);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>display();\nadd(5, 10);</code></pre></div>", "2:Constructors": "<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>A constructor in Java is a special method that is automatically called when an object of a class is created. It is used to initialize the object's data members.</p>\n<h4>Characteristics of Constructors</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Constructor name must be the same as the class name.</li><li>It does not have any return type, not even void.</li><li>It is invoked automatically when an object is created using new.</li><li>Constructors can be overloaded.</li><li>If no constructor is defined, Java provides a default constructor.</li></ul>\n<h4>Types of Constructors</h4>\n<h4>1. Default Constructor</h4>\n<p>A constructor with no parameters.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    Student() {\n        System.out.println(\"Default Constructor Called\");\n    }\n}\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s = new Student();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Parameterized Constructor</h4>\n<p>A constructor that accepts parameters to initialize objects.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    String name;\n    Student(String n) {\n        name = n;\n    }\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(name);\n    }\n}\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s = new Student(\"Rahul\");\n        s.display();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Constructor Overloading</h4>\n<p>A class can have multiple constructors with different parameter lists.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Demo {\n    Demo() {\n        System.out.println(\"No-argument constructor\");\n    }\n    Demo(int x) {\n        System.out.println(\"Parameterized constructor: \" + x);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Advantages of Constructors</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Initializes objects automatically.</li><li>Improves code readability and reusability.</li><li>Ensures objects are created with proper initial values.</li></ul>\n<h4>Conclusion</h4>\n<p>Constructors are special methods used for object initialization in Java. They are automatically executed when an object is created and can be default, parameterized, or overloaded according to program requirements.</p>", "2:The this keyword": "<h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>The this keyword in Java is a reference variable that refers to the current object of a class. It is used to distinguish instance variables from local variables and to access members of the current object.</p>\n<h4>Uses of the this Keyword</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>To Refer to the Current Class Instance Variable</li></ul>\n<p>When local variables and instance variables have the same name, this is used to refer to the instance variable.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    String name;\n    Student(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Name: \" + this.name);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s = new Student(\"Ramesh\");\n        s.display();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Name: Ramesh\n2. To Invoke the Current Class Method</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Demo {\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Display Method\");\n    }\n    void show() {\n        this.display();\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Demo d = new Demo();\n        d.show();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Display Method\n3. To Invoke the Current Class Constructor (Constructor Chaining)</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Test {\n    Test() {\n        this(100);\n        System.out.println(\"Default Constructor\");\n    }\n    Test(int x) {\n        System.out.println(\"Value: \" + x);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new Test();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Value: 100\nDefault Constructor\n4. To Pass the Current Object as an Argument</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Demo {\n    void display(Demo obj) {\n        System.out.println(\"Current object passed.\");\n    }\n    void show() {\n        display(this);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Demo d = new Demo();\n        d.show();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Advantages of the this Keyword</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Eliminates ambiguity between instance and local variables.</li><li>Supports constructor chaining.</li><li>Improves code readability.</li><li>Enables passing or returning the current object.</li><li>Helps access current object members explicitly.</li></ul>\n<h4>Disadvantages of this Keyword in Java</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Cannot be used in static methods.</li><li>May confuse beginners.</li><li>Unnecessary use makes the code less readable.</li><li>Refers only to the current object.</li><li>this() must always be the first statement in a constructor.</li></ul>", "2:Garbage Collection": "<h4>Garbage Collection (GC)</h4>\n<p>Garbage Collection is the process of automatically removing unused objects from heap memory. It is performed by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) to free memory occupied by objects that are no longer referenced by the program.</p>\n<h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>Garbage Collection is an automatic memory management mechanism in Java that identifies and deletes unused objects from the heap, making memory available for new objects.</p>\n<h4>Need for Garbage Collection</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Frees unused memory automatically.</li><li>Prevents memory leaks.</li><li>Improves application performance.</li><li>Eliminates the need for manual memory deallocation.</li><li>Reduces programming errors related to memory management.</li></ul>\n<h4>How Garbage Collection Works</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>An object is created using the new keyword and stored in the heap.</li><li>As long as a reference points to the object, it remains in memory.</li><li>When no references point to the object, it becomes eligible for garbage collection.</li><li>The JVM automatically removes the unused object and reclaims its memory.</li></ul>\n<h4>Ways an Object Becomes Eligible for Garbage Collection</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Nullifying the reference</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Student s = new Student();\ns = null;</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Reassigning the reference</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Student s = new Student();\ns = new Student(); // Object 1 has no reference (it becomes eligible for</code></pre></div>\n<p>garbage collection).</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Anonymous object</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>new Student(); // An object created without storing its reference</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Reference goes out of scope</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Test {</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li></li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>     void display() {\n             Test t = new Test();\n             System.out.println(\"Inside display()\");\n         }</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li></li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>     public static void main(String[] args) {\n             Test obj = new Test();\n             obj.display();</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li></li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>         System.gc();</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li></li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>         System.out.println(\"Method Completed\");\n     }\n }</code></pre></div>\n<p>Note: System.gc() only requests the JVM to perform garbage collection. It does not guarantee immediate execution.</p>\n<h4>Advantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Automatic memory management.</li><li>Prevents memory leaks.</li><li>Reduces programmer effort.</li><li>Efficient utilization of heap memory.</li><li>Improves application reliability.</li></ul>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Cannot force garbage collection.</li><li>Execution time is unpredictable.</li><li>May cause short application pauses.</li><li>finalize() is deprecated and should not be used.</li></ul>", "2:Overloading Methods": "<h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>Method Overloading in Java means defining two or more methods with the same method name but with different parameter lists within the same class. It is an example of Compile-Time Polymorphism (Static Polymorphism).</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class ClassName {\n    void methodName(int a) {\n        // code\n    }\n    void methodName(int a, int b) {\n        // code\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example Program</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Calculator {\n    // Method with two int parameters\n    int add(int a, int b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    // Method with three int parameters\n    int add(int a, int b, int c) {\n        return a + b + c;\n    }\n    // Method with two double parameters\n    double add(double a, double b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Calculator c = new Calculator();\n        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));\n        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20, 30));\n        System.out.println(c.add(10.5, 20.5));\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">30\n60\n31.0</pre>\n<h4>Ways to Achieve Method Overloading</h4>\n<h4>Method overloading can be achieved by changing:</h4>\n<p>1. Number of parameters void display(int a) void display(int a, int b) 2. Data type of parameters void display(int a) void display(double a) 3. Order of parameter types void display(int a, String b) void display(String a, int b)</p>\n<h4>Important Rules</h4>\n<p>The method name must be the same, but the parameter list must be different. Changing only the return type does not achieve method overloading. int sum(int a, int b) double sum(int a, int b) // Error The compiler cannot distinguish these methods because their parameter lists are identical.</p>\n<h4>Advantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Improves code readability.</li><li>The same method name can perform similar operations on different inputs.</li><li>Reduces the need to create different method names.</li><li>Supports compile-time polymorphism.</li><li>Makes programs easier to understand and maintain.</li></ul>", "2:Using Objects as Parameters": "<p>In Java, we can pass an object as an argument to a method, just like passing int, double, or other values.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    int marks;\n    Student(int marks) {\n        this.marks = marks;\n    }\n    void compare(Student s) {\n        if (this.marks &gt; s.marks)\n        System.out.println(\"First student has more marks\");\n        else\n        System.out.println(\"Second student has more marks\");\n    }\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student(90);\n        Student s2 = new Student(80);\n        s1.compare(s2);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">First student has more marks</pre>\n<h4>Here, s2 is an object passed as a parameter to:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>s1.compare(s2);</code></pre></div>\n<p>and the receiving parameter is: void compare(Student s)</p>", "2:Returning Objects": "<p>A method can also return an object. The return type of the method is the class name.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Test {\n    int value;\n    Test(int value) {\n        this.value = value;\n    }\n    Test increase() {\n        Test t = new Test(value + 10);\n        return t;\n    }\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Test t1 = new Test(20);\n        Test t2 = t1.increase();\n        System.out.println(t1.value);\n        System.out.println(t2.value);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">20\n30</pre>\n<h4>Here:</h4>\n<p>Test increase() means the method returns an object of class Test.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>return t;</code></pre></div>\n<p>returns the object, and:</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Test t2 = t1.increase();</code></pre></div>\n<p>stores the returned object's reference in t2.</p>", "2:Understanding static": "<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>The static keyword in Java is used to declare a variable or method that belongs to the class rather than to individual objects.</p>\n<h4>Static Variable Example ::</h4>\n<p>A static member is shared by all objects of the class.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    int rollNo;          // Instance variable\n    String name;           // Instance variable\n    static String college = \"VRSEC\"; // Static variable\n    Student(int r, String n) {\n        rollNo = r;\n        name = n;\n    }\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(rollNo + \" \" + name + \" \" + college);\n    }\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Student s1 = new Student(101, \"Ravi\");\n        Student s2 = new Student(102, \"Kiran\");\n        s1.display();\n        s2.display();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">101 Ravi VRSEC\n102 Kiran VRSEC</pre>\n<h4>Static Method Example ::</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Calculator {\n    static int add(int a, int b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int result = Calculator.add(10, 20);\n        System.out.println(result);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Static vs Non-Static</h4>\n<h4>Static                                  Non-static / Instance</h4>\n<h4>Belongs to class                                Belongs to object</h4>\n<p>One shared copy of static variable              Each object has its own variable Access using class name                         Usually access using object Object creation not required                    Object usually required</p>\n<h4>Example: Student.college                        Example: s1.name</h4>\n<p>Example method: Math.sqrt()                     Example method: s1.display()</p>\n<h4>String Handling –</h4>", "2:Introducing final": "<p>The final keyword in Java is used to restrict changes. Once something is declared final, it cannot be changed or overridden in certain ways.</p>\n<h4>Simple Example – Final Variable</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Test {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        final int x = 10;\n        System.out.println(x);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">10</pre>\n<h4>If we try:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>x = 20;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Java gives a compile-time error, because x is declared final.</p>\n<h4>Three Uses of final</h4>\n<p>Usage                   Meaning final variable       Value cannot be changed Method cannot be overridfinal method den final class          Class cannot be inherited</p>\n<h4>Final Method in Java</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Parent {\n    final void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent method\");\n    }\n}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    // Cannot override show() here\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Child c = new Child();\n        c.show();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Parent method</pre>\n<h4>If we try to override</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Child extends Parent {\n    void show() { // ERROR\n        System.out.println(\"Child method\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>This gives a compile-time error because show() is declared final in the parent class.</p>\n<h4>Final Class in Java</h4>\n<h4>Simple Definition</h4>\n<p>A final class is a class that cannot be inherited by another class.</p>\n<h4>Simple Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>final class Parent {\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello\");\n    }\n}\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Parent p = new Parent();\n        p.show();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Hello</pre>\n<h4>If we try to inherit a final class:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>final class Parent {\n}\nclass Child extends Parent { // ERROR\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>This gives a compile-time error because Parent is declared as final.</p>", "2:Introducing Nested and Inner Classes": "<p>A Nested Class is a class declared inside another class. An Inner Class is a non-static nested class. It can directly access the members of the outer class, including private members.</p>\n<h4>Inner Class example ::</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Outer {\n    int x = 10;\n    class Inner {\n        void display() {\n            System.out.println(\"x = \" + x);\n        }\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Outer obj = new Outer();\n        Outer.Inner in = obj.new Inner();\n        in.display();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">x = 10</pre>\n<h4>Explanation</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Outer → Outer class</li><li>Inner → Inner class declared inside Outer</li><li>x → Variable of the outer class</li><li>Inner can directly access x.</li><li>First, we create an outer object:</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Outer obj = new Outer();</code></pre></div>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Then, we create the inner-class object:</li></ul>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Outer.Inner in = obj.new Inner();</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Nested Class – Static Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Outer {\n    static class Nested {\n        void show() {\n            System.out.println(\"Static Nested Class\");\n        }\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Outer.Nested obj = new Outer.Nested();\n        obj.show();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<h4>Static Nested Class</h4>\n<h4>Nested Class                                       Inner Class</h4>\n<p>A class declared inside another class.            A non-static nested class. Can be static or non-static.                      Always non-static. static nested class does not require an Requires an outer-class object to create it. outer-class object. Static nested class cannot directly access non-   Can directly access all outer-class members, instatic outer members.                             cluding private members.</p>\n<h4>Example: static class B                           Example: class B</h4>", "2:The String Constructors": "<h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>A String is a sequence of characters used to represent text in Java. The String class belongs to the java.lang package. A String constructor is used to create String objects in different ways, such as from character arrays, byte arrays, or other strings.</p>\n<h4>Common String Constructors</h4>\n<h4>1. Empty String Constructor</h4>\n<p>Creates an empty string.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>String s = new String();</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Demo1 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s = new String();\n        System.out.println(\"String = '\" + s + \"'\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">String = ''\n2. Constructor with String Literal</pre>\n<p>Creates a new String object using another string.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>String s = new String(\"Java\");</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Demo2 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s = new String(\"Java\");\n        System.out.println(s);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Java\n3. Constructor with Character Array</pre>\n<p>Creates a string from a character array.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>char ch[] = {'J','A','V','A'};\nString s = new String(ch);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Demo3 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        char ch[] = {'J','A','V','A'};\n        String s = new String(ch);\n        System.out.println(s);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">JAVA\n4. Constructor with Character Array (Partial)</pre>\n<p>Creates a string using a specified portion of the character array.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>String s = new String(ch, offset, count);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Demo4 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        char ch[] = {'P','R','O','G','R','A','M'};\n        String s = new String(ch, 0, 4);\n        System.out.println(s);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">PROG\n5. Constructor with Byte Array</pre>\n<p>Creates a string from a byte array.</p>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>byte b[] = {65,66,67,68};\nString s = new String(b);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Demo5 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        byte b[] = {65,66,67,68};\n        String s = new String(b);\n        System.out.println(s);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">ABCD\n6. Constructor with Byte Array (Partial)</pre>\n<p>Creates a string using part of a byte array.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Demo6 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        byte b[] = {72,69,76,76,79};\n        String s = new String(b, 1, 3);\n        System.out.println(s);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">ELL\n7. Constructor from Another String</pre>\n<p>Creates a new String object from an existing String.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class Demo7 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s1 = \"Programming\";\n        String s2 = new String(s1);\n        System.out.println(s2);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Programming</pre>\n<h4>Summary of String Constructors</h4>\n<h4>Constructor                                         Description</h4>\n<p>String()                                                Creates an empty string String(String s)                                        Creates a string from another string String(char[] ch)                                       Creates a string from a character array String(char[] ch, int offset, int                       Creates a string from part of a character count)                                                  array String(byte[] b)                                        Creates a string from a byte array String(byte[] b, int offset, int length)                                                 Creates a string from part of a byte array</p>\n<h4>Advantages of String Constructors</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Create strings in multiple ways.</li><li>Support character arrays and byte arrays.</li><li>Easy conversion of arrays into strings.</li><li>Useful for text processing and data conversion.</li></ul>", "2:StringBuffer Class": "<h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>The StringBuffer class in Java is used to create mutable (modifiable) string objects. Unlike the String class, the contents of a StringBuffer object can be changed without creating a new object.</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Package: java.lang</li><li>Mutable: Yes</li></ul>\n<h4>Features of StringBuffer</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Mutable – contents can be modified.</li><li>Thread-safe because methods are synchronized.</li><li>Better performance than String when performing multiple string modifications.</li><li>Automatically increases its capacity when required.</li><li>Supports methods like append(), insert(), delete(), replace(), reverse(), etc.</li></ul>\n<h4>Constructors of StringBuffer</h4>\n<h4>1. Default Constructor</h4>\n<p>Creates an empty StringBuffer with an initial capacity of 16 characters.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer();</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Constructor with Capacity</h4>\n<p>Creates an empty StringBuffer with the specified capacity.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer(50);</code></pre></div>\n<h4>3. Constructor with String</h4>\n<p>Creates a StringBuffer initialized with the given string.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer(\"Java\");</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Common Methods of StringBuffer</h4>\n<p>Method            Description append() Adds text at the end insert() Inserts text at a specified position Method                   Description delete()         Removes characters replace()        Replaces characters reverse()        Reverses the string length()         Returns the number of characters capacity()       Returns current capacity Returns character at specified incharAt() dex setCharAt( Changes a character ) toString() Converts StringBuffer to String</p>\n<h4>Example Program</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class StringBufferDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer(\"Java\");\n        sb.append(\" Programming\");\n        sb.insert(5, \"Language \");\n        sb.replace(0, 4, \"Core\");\n        sb.delete(5, 14);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        System.out.println(\"Length = \" + sb.length());\n        System.out.println(\"Capacity = \" + sb.capacity());\n        sb.reverse();\n        System.out.println(\"Reverse = \" + sb);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Core Programming\nLength = 16\nCapacity = 20\nReverse = gnimmargorP eroC</pre>\n<h4>Advantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Faster than String for repeated modifications.</li><li>Memory efficient because it modifies the same object.</li><li>Thread-safe due to synchronized methods.</li><li>Automatically expands its capacity.</li></ul>\n<h4>Disadvantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Slightly slower than StringBuilder because of synchronization.</li><li>Consumes more memory than String for small, fixed strings.</li></ul>\n<p>********************************************************************************</p>\n<h4>1. Immutable (String)</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class ImmutableExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s = \"Java\";\n        s.concat(\" Programming\"); // Creates a new object\n        System.out.println(s);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Java\n2. Mutable (StringBuffer)</pre>\n<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>A mutable object can be changed after it is created. No new object is created during modification.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>public class MutableExample {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer(\"Java\");\n        sb.append(\" Programming\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Java Programming</pre>", "2:StringTokenizer class": "<h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>The StringTokenizer class is used to split a string into smaller parts called tokens based on a delimiter (separator).</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Package: java.util</li><li>A token is a word or part of a string separated by a delimiter.</li><li>Default delimiter: Space (\" \")</li></ul>\n<h4>Features of StringTokenizer</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Splits a string into tokens.</li><li>Uses delimiters such as space, comma, colon, etc.</li><li>Default delimiter is whitespace.</li><li>Easy and fast for simple string parsing.</li><li>Belongs to the java.util package.</li></ul>\n<h4>Constructors</h4>\n<h4>1. Using Default Delimiter (Space)</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(\"Java is Easy\");</code></pre></div>\n<h4>2. Using a Custom Delimiter</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(\"Java,C,Python\", \",\");</code></pre></div>\n<h4>3. Returning Delimiters as Tokens</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(\"A,B,C\", \",\", true);</code></pre></div>\n<p>Here, true means the delimiter (,) is also returned as a token.</p>\n<h4>Common Methods</h4>\n<p>Method                            Description hasMoreTokens()           Returns true if more tokens are available nextToken()               Returns the next token countTokens()             Returns the number of remaining tokens hasMoreElements()         Checks if more elements exist nextElement()             Returns the next element as an Object</p>\n<h4>Example 1: Default Delimiter</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>import java.util.StringTokenizer;\npublic class TokenDemo1 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String str = \"Java Programming Language\";\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(str);\n        while (st.hasMoreTokens()) {\n            System.out.println(st.nextToken());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Java\nProgramming\nLanguage</pre>\n<h4>Example 2: Comma Delimiter</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>import java.util.StringTokenizer;\npublic class TokenDemo2 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String str = \"Java,C++,Python,PHP\";\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(str, \",\");\n        while (st.hasMoreTokens()) {\n            System.out.println(st.nextToken());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Java\nC++\nPython\nPHP</pre>\n<h4>Advantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Simple and easy to use.</li><li>Faster for basic string splitting.</li><li>Supports different delimiters.</li><li>Uses less memory for simple tokenization.</li></ul>\n<h4>Disadvantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>It is a legacy class.</li><li>Less flexible than the String.split() method.</li><li>Does not support regular expressions.</li></ul>\n<h4>StringTokenizer vs String.split()</h4>\n<h4>StringTokenizer                  String.split()</h4>\n<h4>Legacy class                  Modern method</h4>\n<h4>Uses regular expressions</h4>\n<p>Uses delimiters</p>\n<h4>(regex)</h4>\n<h4>Faster for simple tokeniza-</h4>\n<p>More flexible tion Returns tokens one by one     Returns an array of strings</p>\n<h4>Applications</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Reading words from a sentence.</li><li>Parsing CSV (comma-separated) values.</li><li>Processing user input.</li><li>Splitting data using delimiters.</li></ul>", "2:Inheritance Basics": "<h4>Definition</h4>\n<p>Inheritance is an Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) feature in which one class acquires the properties (fields) and behaviors (methods) of another class.</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>The existing class is called the Superclass (Parent/Base class).</li><li>The new class is called the Subclass (Child/Derived class).</li><li>Java uses the extends keyword for inheritance.</li></ul>\n<h4>Advantages</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Code reusability</li><li>Reduces code duplication</li><li>Supports method overriding</li><li>Enables polymorphism</li><li>Easy maintenance</li></ul>\n<h4>Syntax</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Parent {\n    // fields and methods\n}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    // additional fields and methods\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal is eating\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog is barking\");\n    }\n}\npublic class Test {\n    public static void main(String args[]) {\n        Dog d = new Dog();\n        d.eat();\n        d.bark();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Animal is eating\nDog is barking</pre>\n<h4>Types of inheritance in java</h4>\n<p>In Java, inheritance allows one class to acquire the properties and methods of another class using the extends keyword.</p>\n<h4>Types of Inheritance in Java</h4>\n<p>Type                              Meaning                      Supported with Classes?</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Single Inheritance        One child inherits from one parent            ✅ Yes</li><li>Multilevel Inheritance    A class inherits from another derived class ✅ Yes</li><li>Hierarchical Inheri-      Multiple child classes inherit from one</li></ul>\n<p>✅ Yes tance                        parent</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Multiple Inheritance      One class inherits from multiple classes ❌ No</li><li>Hybrid Inheritance        Combination of two or more types              ❌ Not directly with classes</li></ul>\n<h4>1. Single Inheritance</h4>\n<p>One child class inherits from one parent class.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal eats\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\nclass Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Dog d = new Dog();\n        d.eat();   // inherited method\n        d.bark();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Structure:</h4>\n<p>Animal</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<h4>Dog</h4>\n<h4>2. Multilevel Inheritance</h4>\n<p>A class inherits from a class, which itself inherits from another class.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Eating\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() {\n        System.out.println(\"Barking\");\n    }\n}\nclass Puppy extends Dog {\n    void play() {\n        System.out.println(\"Playing\");\n    }\n}\nclass Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Puppy p = new Puppy();\n        p.eat();\n        p.bark();\n        p.play();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Structure:</h4>\n<p>Animal</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>Dog</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<h4>Puppy</h4>\n<h4>3. Hierarchical Inheritance</h4>\n<p>Multiple child classes inherit from the same parent class.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal eats\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\nclass Cat extends Animal {\n    void meow() {\n        System.out.println(\"Cat meows\");\n    }\n}\nclass Demo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Dog d = new Dog();\n        d.eat();\n        d.bark();\n        Cat c = new Cat();\n        c.eat();\n        c.meow();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Structure:</h4>\n<p>Animal /    \\</p>\n<h4>↓                   ↓</h4>\n<h4>Dog                   Cat</h4>\n<h4>4. Multiple Inheritance</h4>\n<p>One class trying to inherit from two classes. Java does not support multiple inheritance using classes.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class A {\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"A\");\n    }\n}\nclass B {\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"B\");\n    }\n}\n// ❌ Not allowed in Java\nclass C extends A, B {\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>This is avoided because it can create ambiguity. For example, if both A and B have a show() method, Java would not know which one C should inherit.</p>\n<h4>However, Java can achieve multiple inheritance through interfaces:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>interface A {\n    void showA();\n}\ninterface B {\n    void showB();\n}\nclass C implements A, B {\n    public void showA() {\n        System.out.println(\"A\");\n    }\n    public void showB() {\n        System.out.println(\"B\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Structure:</h4>\n<p>A                     B \\             /</p>\n<h4>\\     /</h4>\n<h4>C</h4>\n<h4>5. Hybrid Inheritance</h4>\n<p>Hybrid inheritance is a combination of different inheritance types. Java does not support hybrid inheritance directly through classes, but it can be achieved using interfaces.</p>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<p>Animal</p>\n<h4>/    \\</h4>\n<p>Dog     Cat</p>\n<h4>|</h4>\n<h4>Puppy</h4>\n<p>This combines hierarchical and multilevel inheritance.</p>\n<h4>Easy way to remember</h4>\n<h4>Single          : A → B</h4>\n<h4>Multilevel      : A → B → C</h4>\n<p>Hierarchical :               A</p>\n<h4>/ \\</h4>\n<h4>B         C</h4>\n<h4>Multiple        : A + B → C           ❌ Classes</h4>\n<h4>Hybrid          : Combination of inheritance types</h4>\n<p>Important for exams: Java supports Single, Multilevel, and Hierarchical inheritance with classes. Multiple and Hybrid inheritance are not directly supported with classes, but similar behavior can be achieved using interfaces.</p>", "2:Using super": "<p>The super keyword refers to the immediate parent class.</p>\n<h4>Uses of super</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Access parent class variables.</li><li>Call parent class methods.</li><li>Invoke parent class constructor.</li></ul>\n<h4>(a) Access Parent Variable</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class A {\n    int x = 100;\n}\nclass B extends A {\n    int x = 200;\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(super.x);\n        System.out.println(x);\n    }\n}\npublic class Test {\n    public static void main(String args[]) {\n        B obj = new B();\n        obj.display();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">100\n200\n(b) Call Parent Method</pre>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Parent {\n    void show() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent Method\");\n    }\n}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    void show() {\n        super.show();\n        System.out.println(\"Child Method\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>(c) Call Parent Constructor</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Parent {\n    Parent() {\n        System.out.println(\"Parent Constructor\");\n    }\n}\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    Child() {\n        super();\n        System.out.println(\"Child Constructor\");\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Parent Constructor\nChild Constructor</pre>", "2:Creating a Multilevel Hierarchy": "<p>In Multilevel Inheritance, one class inherits another class, which is inherited by another class.</p>\n<h4>Hierarchy</h4>\n<p>Animal</p>\n<h4>↑</h4>\n<p>Dog</p>\n<h4>↑</h4>\n<h4>Puppy</h4>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Eating...\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void bark() {\n        System.out.println(\"Barking...\");\n    }\n}\nclass Puppy extends Dog {\n    void weep() {\n        System.out.println(\"Playing...\");\n    }\n}\npublic class Test {\n    public static void main(String args[]) {\n        Puppy p = new Puppy();\n        p.eat();\n        p.bark();\n        p.weep();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<p>Eating... Barking... Playing...</p>", "2:Method Overriding": "<p>Method overriding means redefining the parent class method in the child class using the same method signature.</p>\n<h4>Rules</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Same method name.</li><li>Same parameters.</li><li>Return type must be same or covariant.</li><li>Runtime polymorphism is achieved.</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal makes sound\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\npublic class Test {\n    public static void main(String args[]) {\n        Dog d = new Dog();\n        d.sound();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Dog barks</pre>", "2:Dynamic Method Dispatch": "<p>Dynamic Method Dispatch is a mechanism in Java where a method call to an overridden method is resolved at runtime, rather than at compile time. It is one of the most important features of runtime polymorphism in inheritance.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal makes a sound\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\nclass Cat extends Animal {\n    @Override\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Cat meows\");\n    }\n}\npublic class DynamicDispatchDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Animal a;            // Parent class reference\n        a = new Dog(); // Reference points to Dog object\n        a.sound();    // Dog's sound() is called\n        a = new Cat(); // Reference points to Cat object\n        a.sound();     // Cat's sound() is called\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Dog barks\nCat meows</pre>\n<h4>How it works</h4>\n<h4>The important statement is:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Animal a = new Dog();</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Here:</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Animal → reference type</li><li>Dog → actual object type</li><li>sound() → overridden method</li></ul>\n<h4>When:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>a.sound();</code></pre></div>\n<p>is executed, Java determines at runtime which sound() method should execute. Since the actual object is Dog, the Dog version is called.</p>\n<h4>Then:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>a = new Cat();\na.sound();</code></pre></div>\n<p>calls the Cat version.</p>\n<h4>Important conditions</h4>\n<h4>Dynamic Method Dispatch requires:</h4>\n<h4>1. Inheritance</h4>\n<h4>2. Method overriding</h4>\n<h4>3. Parent-class reference</h4>\n<h4>4. Child-class object</h4>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>The method call must be to an overridden instance method</li></ul>\n<h4>Example pattern:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Parent ref;\nref = new Child1();\nref.method();    // Child1 method\nref = new Child2();\nref.method();    // Child2 method</code></pre></div>\n<p>Same reference and same method call, but different behavior depending on the actual object. That is Dynamic Method Dispatch.</p>", "2:Using Abstract Classes": "<p>An abstract class is a class declared with the abstract keyword. It can contain:</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>Abstract methods — methods without a body</li><li>Concrete methods — methods with a body</li><li>Variables and constructors</li></ul>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>abstract class Animal {\n    // Abstract method\n    abstract void sound();\n    // Concrete method\n    void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal is eating\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    // Providing implementation for abstract method\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\npublic class AbstractDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Dog d = new Dog();\n        d.sound();\n        d.eat();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Dog barks\nAnimal is eating</pre>\n<p>Important points abstract class Animal ↓ extends ↓ class Dog</p>\n<ul class=\"k\"><li>We cannot create an object of an abstract class directly.</li><li>A subclass must implement its abstract methods.</li><li>Abstract classes are useful when we want to provide common functionality while forcing subclasses to provide specific implementations.</li></ul>\n<h4>For example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>Animal a = new Dog(); // Valid\n// Animal a = new Animal(); // Invalid</code></pre></div>", "2:Using final with Inheritance": "<p>The final keyword can be used with classes, methods, and variables.</p>\n<h4>A. Final Class</h4>\n<p>A final class cannot be inherited.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>final class Vehicle {\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Vehicle class\");\n    }\n}\n// This is NOT allowed\n// class Car extends Vehicle {\n    // }</code></pre></div>\n<h4>For example:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>final class Vehicle {\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Vehicle\");\n    }\n}\npublic class FinalClassDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Vehicle v = new Vehicle();\n        v.display();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>B. Final Method</h4>\n<p>A final method cannot be overridden by a subclass.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    final void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal eats\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    // ERROR: Cannot override final method\n    // void eat() {\n        // System.out.println(\"Dog eats\");\n        // }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>But the subclass can inherit and use the method:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Animal {\n    final void eat() {\n        System.out.println(\"Animal eats\");\n    }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    void sound() {\n        System.out.println(\"Dog barks\");\n    }\n}\npublic class FinalMethodDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Dog d = new Dog();\n        d.eat();\n        d.sound();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"ol\">Output</div>\n<pre class=\"out\">Animal eats\nDog barks\n3. Final Variable</pre>\n<p>A final variable can be assigned only once.</p>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>class Student {\n    final int rollNo = 101;\n    void display() {\n        System.out.println(\"Roll No: \" + rollNo);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h4>This is invalid:</h4>\n<div class=\"cb\"><button class=\"cp\">Copy</button><pre><code>rollNo = 102; // ERROR</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Quick comparison</h4>\n<p>Usage                Meaning final class         Cannot be inherited final method        Cannot be overridden final variable      Cannot be reassigned Cannot be directly instantiabstract class ated</p>\n<h4>Usage                   Meaning</h4>\n<p>Must be implemented by abstract method subclass</p>\n<h4>Simple real-world idea</h4>\n<p>Abstract class</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>Provides a common structure</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>Subclass must complete it final class</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>Stops inheritance final method</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<p>Stops method overriding final variable</p>\n<h4>↓</h4>\n<h4>Stops value modification</h4>"};
